<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bagratid Armenia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 884–1045</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Bagratuni kingdom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bagratid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was an independent Armenian state established by Ashot I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratuni dynasty</a> in the early 880s, after nearly two centuries of Umayyad and Abbasid rule over Greater Armenia. Courted by Byzantine and Arab leaders who wanted a buffer state, Ashot was recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> as prince of princes in 862 and as king in 884 or 885. Under Ashot III, Ani became the capital and a thriving economic and cultural centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> annexed parts of the southwest, King Hovhannes-Smbat pledged in 1022 that the kingdom would pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and his successor Gagik II resisted until Byzantine forces took it in 1045.</p></div>
